1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,500
A simulated compromised agent asks for $4.99, 2.5x its $2.00 cap, and claims it is allowed.

2
00:00:04,500 --> 00:00:11,000
The NEAR contract says no, and journals it on-chain with a verdict_override event.

3
00:00:46,000 --> 00:00:52,000
A brand-new guard deployed for this video. The agent must ask it before every trade.

4
00:00:52,000 --> 00:00:57,500
A real NEAR Intents 1Click quote: worst-case slippage has to fit the on-chain policy.

5
00:00:57,500 --> 00:01:02,400
Every decision is a real testnet transaction, journaled by the contract.

6
00:01:02,400 --> 00:01:08,400
Allowed and journaled. The contract computes the paper result itself (here −$0.02). The cheat: rejected.

7
00:01:08,400 --> 00:01:15,400
The dashboard reads every decision straight from the contract.

8
00:01:15,400 --> 00:01:22,400
Signal, guard verdict, quote, where the funds are, and the outcome.

9
00:01:22,400 --> 00:01:28,900
The bot's only key: a function-call key for one contract and two journal methods.

10
00:01:28,900 --> 00:01:31,900
It can't move funds, pause, or change the rules.

11
00:01:31,900 --> 00:01:40,100
The owner sends one pause transaction to the guard.

12
00:01:40,100 --> 00:01:43,600
The next check returns PAUSED. Resume, and it is OK again.

13
00:01:43,600 --> 00:01:53,600
At the $1 daily loss cap, the contract pauses itself.

14
00:01:53,600 --> 00:01:56,600
Proven by a sandbox test on a local NEAR node.

15
00:01:56,600 --> 00:02:03,600
Every fee, the gas and a 31.2% tax reserve are counted before anything is called a profit.

16
00:02:03,600 --> 00:02:10,600
Wins and losses are both shown. These are paper trades, and they are labelled paper.

17
00:02:10,600 --> 00:02:24,350
Don't trust us either: clone it and run two commands. No keys, no funds.

18
00:02:24,350 --> 00:02:26,850
Setup + demo: 30 seconds on this machine.

19
00:02:26,850 --> 00:02:33,450
Then the full test suites.

20
00:02:33,450 --> 00:02:35,950
388 Python tests and 40 + 2 contract tests pass.
